
This directory holds the experiment firmwares of the project. They are not
PlatformIO unit tests (see `test_ignore` in platformio.ini).


More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html


test_tx_power

Purpose
-------
Test for section 5.4 of the assignment: "Changing transmission power to
evaluate energy consumption and signal strength". It measures how signal
strength (RSSI) and power consumption change with the LoRa transmission power.

What it does
------------
It runs on the TRANSMITTER board. First it initialises LoRa with
LoRaIOT_setup() and the values in config.h. Then, for each power level in the
list (2, 5, 8, 11, 14, 17 and 20 dBm):

  1. Sets the power with LoRa.setTxPower().
  2. Sends 10 packets, 500 ms apart. Each packet is a JSON with the power
     used and a sequence number:
         {"test":"tx_power","power":14,"seq":3}
  3. Measures how long each send takes (time on air) and prints a CSV line:
         power_dBm,sent,failed,avg_time_on_air_ms
         14,10,0,30.0
  4. Waits 3 s before moving to the next power level.

When it finishes, it restores the power from config.h (A3MESH_POWER).

The test PASSES if LoRa initialises correctly and every packet is transmitted.

How to use it
-------------
1. RECEIVER board: upload the normal program (env rx) and open its serial
   monitor. Nothing needs to change; it prints the RSSI of every packet:
         Received '{"test":"tx_power","power":14,"seq":3}' with RSSI -52

2. TRANSMITTER board: connect it and run the test:
         pio test -e tx_power
   (or in VS Code: select env:tx_power in the bottom bar and click the
    Test button, or PlatformIO icon > tx_power > Advanced > Test)

3. For power consumption, put a USB power meter (or a multimeter in series)
   on the transmitter's power supply and write down the current for each
   power level. The 3 s pauses between levels give you time to note it.

How to compute the results
--------------------------
- Signal strength: average RSSI per power level (from the receiver's monitor).
- Energy per packet:   E = V · I · t_air
      V      = supply voltage (5 V over USB)
      I      = current measured at that power level
      t_air  = avg_time_on_air_ms from the CSV
  The time on air is the same for every power level (it only depends on SF,
  bandwidth and packet size), so the energy changes only with the current.

Parameters (at the top of test_tx_power/test_tx_power.cpp)
-----------------------------------------------------------
  TX_POWERS          power levels to test, in dBm
  PACKETS_PER_LEVEL  packets per power level (10)
  PACKET_GAP_MS      pause between packets (500 ms)
  LEVEL_GAP_MS       pause between power levels (3000 ms)

Notes
-----
- During the test the receiver publishes temp=0 and hum=0 to MQTT, because
  these packets carry no temperature or humidity.
- At 868 MHz the legal limit is usually 14 dBm. Use 17 and 20 dBm only for
  the lab test, with the boards close together.
- Both boards must have the same LoRa settings (frequency, SF, BW, CR and
  sync word) in config.h.






























































































range_speed_balance_bw

"Adjusting the bandwidth and data rate to balance transmission speed and range."

One TX board and one RX board (Heltec WiFi LoRa 32 V2) sweep these LoRa
configs, CR 4/5 fixed:

    BW {125, 250, 500} kHz  x  SF {7, 9, 12}   ->  9 configs

Both boards sync their clock through WiFi/NTP and switch configs at the same
time, without any handshake. Each config owns a time window aligned to the
clock. In that window TX sends 10 packets `{"id":N,"t":"HH:MM:SS.mmm"}`.

The send interval follows the EU868 1% duty cycle (interval = airtime / 0.01,
5 s minimum), so slow configs take longer:

    BW (kHz)  SF   airtime   interval   window
    125        7    67 ms      6.7 s     70 s
    125        9   226 ms     22.6 s    229 s
    125       12  1647 ms    164.7 s   1650 s
    250        7    33 ms      5.0 s     53 s
    250        9   113 ms     11.3 s    116 s
    250       12   823 ms     82.3 s    826 s
    500        7    17 ms      5.0 s     53 s
    500        9    57 ms      5.7 s     60 s
    500       12   371 ms     37.1 s    374 s

A full cycle takes about 57 min. Both boards print this table at boot.


How to use it
-------------

1. Set `WIFI_SSID` / `WIFI_PASSWORD` in `include/config.h`. Both boards need
   internet access for NTP.
2. Flash each board:

       pio run -e range_speed_bw_tx -t upload
       pio run -e range_speed_bw_rx -t upload

3. Open the serial monitor of both boards (`pio device monitor`, 115200 baud)
   and save the output, e.g. `pio device monitor | tee rx_10m.log`.
4. Let it run for at least one full cycle (~57 min). A board started in the
   middle of a window only sends/expects the remaining slots of that window.
5. Repeat the cycle at different distances (e.g. 1 m, 10 m, 50 m, through
   walls...) to see where each config stops receiving.

Parameters (`PACKETS_PER_CONFIG`, `GUARD_MS`, `EXPERIMENT_CONFIGS`, ...) are
at the top of `range_speed_balance_bw/main.cpp`. Both boards must run the same
values, or they will not be in the same config at the same time.


Output
------

TX, one line per packet:

    [TX|11:44:30.123] {"id":3,"t":"11:44:30.123"} | airtime 67.9 ms (theory 66.8 ms)

RX, one line per packet and a summary at the end of each window:

    [RX|-80dBm|11:44:30.192] {"id":3,"t":"11:44:30.123"} | SNR 9.5 dB | delta 69 ms (theory 66.8 ms)
    --- Summary BW 125.0 kHz SF 7 | received 10/10 (loss 0%) | avg RSSI -80.2 dBm | avg SNR 9.4 dB | avg delta 68.7 ms ---


How to evaluate the results
---------------------------

Speed
  - `airtime` (TX) is the real time-on-air and should be close to `theory`.
  - `delta` (RX) = t_rx - t_tx. It should match the airtime; the difference is
    the NTP sync error between both boards (a few ms up to ~20 ms). Fast
    configs (SF7 / 500 kHz, ~17 ms) are the most affected by that error.
  - Effective data rate = payload bits / airtime (27 bytes = 216 bits).
  - Expected trend: doubling BW halves the airtime; each SF step roughly
    doubles it. SF7/500 kHz is the fastest, SF12/125 kHz the slowest (~100x).

Range
  - RSSI: received power. Closer to 0 dBm means stronger signal.
  - SNR: LoRa can decode below the noise floor (negative SNR). The demodulation
    limit is about -7.5 dB for SF7 and -20 dB for SF12. A larger margin between
    the measured SNR and that limit means more range left.
  - Packet loss (summary): the practical range limit is where loss grows.
  - Expected trend: higher SF and lower BW reach further. Each SF step gives
    about +2.5 dB of sensitivity and halving BW about +3 dB.

Trade-off
  - Put every config in a table: airtime, data rate, avg RSSI, avg SNR, loss,
    one table per distance.
  - At short distance all configs receive everything, so the fastest one wins.
    As the distance grows, the fast configs lose packets first. The best
    balance is the fastest config that still keeps the loss near 0% at the
    target distance.
  - Remember the cost of slow configs: with a 1% duty cycle, SF12/125 kHz can
    send only one packet every ~165 s.
